Skill loaded and routed. Classification: **3D + plain JS/Vite** → specialist path `three-webgl-game`, with `game-ui-frontend` (HUD budget) and `game-playtest` (QA gate) folded in. Three of that skill's defaults are overridden by your standing Mission Directive, which wins:

| `three-webgl-game` default | Mission Directive | Resolution |
|---|---|---|
| TypeScript | Vanilla JS + ES Modules | **Directive** — JSDoc typedefs used for editor-level type safety instead |
| Rapier JS for physics | Hand-written vanilla physics | **Directive** — swept-AABB + lattice-inverse broadphase, documented below |
| GLB / glTF 2.0 + `GLTFLoader`/`DRACOLoader`/`KTX2Loader` | 100% procedural assets | **Directive** — `render/loaders/` is replaced by `procgen/`; zero loaders, zero `public/` bytes |

Everything else from the skill is adopted verbatim and is load-bearing in this plan: simulation state lives **outside** Three.js objects; the render graph is an adapter; camera behavior is an explicit boundary; UI is DOM; the HUD is **low-chrome** (one objective chip, one status strip, transient prompts — no four-corner glass dashboard); resize and **WebGL context loss** are treated as real browser concerns; post-processing is measurable and never allowed to hide gameplay readability.

---

# `src/games/Space_Invaders/plan.md`

# SPACE INVADERS — *NEON BULWARK*
### AAA Retro-Futurism Implementation Plan · Difficulty ★★★☆☆

> **Fantasy:** You are the last autonomous defence platform on a dead orbital ring. An alien lattice descends in perfect, arrogant lockstep. It accelerates as it dies.
> **Player verbs:** Strafe. Fire. Hide. Read the rhythm.
> **Session length:** 4–9 minutes to loss; wave 8+ is the skill ceiling.
> **Core tension:** The march tempo *is* the difficulty curve, the soundtrack, and the timer — all three are the same number.

---

## 0. DESIGN NORTH STAR

The 1978 original has one perfect accident: the CPU updated exactly **one invader per frame**, so the formation's step period was proportional to the number of survivors. Killing invaders literally made the game faster and the music quicker. Nothing was scripted; the difficulty curve was an emergent property of the render loop.

**We reproduce that accident deliberately, as the spine of the entire title.** One scalar, `formation.stepPeriod`, drives:

- formation march tempo
- the bass-line sequencer BPM (`MusicDirector`)
- bomb-drop probability per step
- bloom pulse intensity on the arena grid
- camera dolly-in pressure

Every modern enhancement below is layered *on top of* that scalar. Nothing is allowed to break it.

---

## 1. CORE GAMEPLAY — MATHEMATICAL MODEL

### 1.1 World Space & Units

All simulation runs in a single XY plane at `z = 0`, camera looking down `-Z` with a slight tilt. One "world unit" ≈ one invader half-width.

| Constant | Value | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `ARENA_W` | 30.0 | playfield width |
| `ARENA_H` | 22.0 | playfield height |
| `ARENA_HALF_W` | 15.0 | |
| `PLAYER_Y` | −9.0 | ship rail |
| `KILL_LINE_Y` | −7.6 | formation reaching this = instant loss |
| `SPAWN_TOP_Y` | 7.5 | wave 1 formation origin Y |
| `COLS` × `ROWS` | 11 × 5 | 55 invaders, canonical |
| `SPACING_X` | 1.90 | lattice pitch |
| `SPACING_Y` | 1.55 | lattice pitch |
| `INVADER_HALF` | (0.65, 0.48) | AABB half-extents |
| `STEP_X` | 0.42 | lateral translation per march step |
| `DROP_Y` | 0.70 | descent per wall reversal |
| `FIXED_DT` | 1/120 s | simulation tick |

### 1.2 Formation as a Rigid Body

The 55 invaders are **never** stored as 55 independent positions. They are a lattice + one origin:

```
worldPos(i) = origin + ( col(i) * SPACING_X , -row(i) * SPACING_Y ) + bob(i)
alive: Uint8Array(55)
col(i) = i % 11 ,  row(i) = (i / 11) | 0
```

`bob(i)` is a **visual-only** offset (§3.4) and is excluded from all collision math. This is the skill's "simulation state outside Three.js objects" rule applied at the sharpest point: `InstancedMesh` matrices are derived per frame from `origin`, never read back.

**Live bounds** are maintained incrementally. `minAliveCol` / `maxAliveCol` / `maxAliveRow` are recomputed **only on a death event** (dirty flag), by scanning the 55-byte array — 55 byte-reads a few times a second, not per frame.

### 1.3 The March — Authentic Acceleration

```
stepPeriod = clamp( aliveCount * FRAME_BUDGET * levelScalar , MIN_PERIOD , MAX_PERIOD )

FRAME_BUDGET = 1/60          // one invader per frame, 1978
levelScalar  = 1 / (1 + 0.085 * (level - 1))
MIN_PERIOD   = 0.055         // ~18 steps/sec ceiling — readable, not seizure-inducing
MAX_PERIOD   = 0.95
```

At 55 alive, wave 1: `55/60 = 0.917 s`. At 1 alive: clamped to `0.055 s`. **16.7× acceleration, derived, not authored.**

March integration uses an accumulator so it is frame-rate independent and survives hit-stop:

```
marchAccum += scaledDt
while (marchAccum >= stepPeriod) { marchAccum -= stepPeriod; doMarchStep(); }
```

### 1.4 Wall Reversal — With the Latch

Naïve reversal double-flips when a formation is wide enough to touch both walls, freezing it. Guard with a latch:

```
doMarchStep():
  nextOriginX = origin.x + dir * STEP_X
  leftEdge  = nextOriginX + minAliveCol*SPACING_X - INVADER_HALF.x
  rightEdge = nextOriginX + maxAliveCol*SPACING_X + INVADER_HALF.x

  if ((rightEdge > ARENA_HALF_W && dir > 0) || (leftEdge < -ARENA_HALF_W && dir < 0)):
      dir = -dir
      origin.y -= DROP_Y          // drop and reverse on the SAME step (canonical)
      onFormationDrop()           // -> trauma 0.06, bass note pitch-down, grid pulse
  else:
      origin.x = nextOriginX

  animFrame ^= 1                  // two-pose leg toggle (§3.2)
  maybeDropBomb()
  Audio.marchTick(aliveCount)
```

The directional guard (`&& dir > 0`) is the latch: an edge already past the wall cannot re-trigger.

### 1.5 Player Kinematics

Classic uses instant constant velocity. We keep the *feel* crisp but add frame-rate-independent exponential smoothing so gamepad analog input reads properly:

```
targetV = axisX * PLAYER_MAX_V              // PLAYER_MAX_V = 13.5 u/s
v      += (targetV - v) * (1 - exp(-RESPONSE * dt))    // RESPONSE = 26
x       = clamp(x + v*dt, -ARENA_HALF_W + 1.0, ARENA_HALF_W - 1.0)
```

`1 - exp(-k·dt)` (not `k·dt`) is the correct frame-rate-independent damp — mandatory since we render decoupled from the 120 Hz sim.

Visual bank: `rollTarget = -clamp(v/PLAYER_MAX_V,-1,1) * 0.34 rad`, damped at `k=14`. Thruster plume scale = `0.6 + 0.5*|v|/PLAYER_MAX_V`.

### 1.6 Projectiles & Swept Collision

Player bullet speed is 34 u/s. At `FIXED_DT` that is 0.283 u per tick against an invader half-height of 0.48 — safe, but bombs at 18 u/s vs the player's 0.22 half-height are **not**. All bullet↔target tests are therefore **swept**, never point-in-box:

```
// segment (p0 -> p1) vs AABB, slab method
sweptSegmentAABB(p0, p1, center, half):
    d = p1 - p0
    tEnter = 0, tExit = 1
    for axis in (x, y):
        if |d[axis]| < EPS:
            if |p0[axis] - center[axis]| > half[axis]: return null
        else:
            inv = 1/d[axis]
            t1 = (center[axis] - half[axis] - p0[axis]) * inv
            t2 = (center[axis] + half[axis] - p0[axis]) * inv
            if t1 > t2: swap(t1,t2)
            tEnter = max(tEnter,t1); tExit = min(tExit,t2)
            if tEnter > tExit: return null
    return tEnter                  // exact impact point = p0 + d*tEnter
```

The returned `tEnter` gives the **exact** impact point, which is what the VFX layer uses to place sparks and shockwaves — impacts land on the surface, not inside it.

### 1.7 Broadphase — Lattice Inversion (O(1))

Because invaders live on a perfect lattice, a bullet does not need a spatial hash. It inverse-maps into lattice coordinates directly:

```
local = bulletMidpoint - origin
col   = round(local.x / SPACING_X)
row   = round(-local.y / SPACING_Y)
if (col in [0,10] && row in [0,4]):
    idx = row*11 + col
    if alive[idx]: sweptTest(idx)      // plus the 2 vertically adjacent cells for fast bullets
```

**One candidate cell (three when swept vertically), not 55 tests.** This is the single highest-leverage performance decision in the game and it falls straight out of respecting the original's lattice.

### 1.8 Bombs

Canonical: only the **bottom-most living invader of a column** may fire. Maintained as an incremental `bottomOfColumn: Int8Array(11)`, patched on death in O(rows).

```
pFire(step) = clamp( BASE_P * (1 + 0.14*(level-1)) * (1 + 0.9*(1 - alive/55)) , 0 , 0.42 )
BASE_P = 0.055
maxConcurrentBombs = min(2 + floor(level/2), 6)
```

Three bomb archetypes, each a distinct trajectory function of local time `τ`:

| Type | Trajectory | Speed | Destructible by bullet |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Plunger** | `x = x₀`, straight | 15.0 | yes |
| **Squiggly** | `x = x₀ + 0.42·sin(9τ)` | 13.5 | yes |
| **Rolling** (targeting) | `x += clamp(playerX − x, −2.4, 2.4)·dt` | 17.5 | **no** — the threat you must dodge |

The rolling bomb being bullet-proof is the original's rule and is what forces lateral movement instead of turret camping. Preserved.

### 1.9 Bunkers — Voxel Destruction

Four bunkers at `x ∈ {−9.6, −3.2, 3.2, 9.6}`, `y = −6.3`. Each is a **22 × 16 occupancy grid** (`Uint8Array(352)`), cell size 0.115 u, rendered as **one `InstancedMesh` per bunker** (max 352 instances, typically ~230 after the arch cut-out).

Initial shape from an analytic mask (dome + arch), matching the arcade silhouette:

```
occupied(cx, cy):
    nx = (cx - 10.5)/10.5 ;  ny = cy/15
    if (ny > 0.62 && nx*nx > (1 - ((ny-0.62)/0.38)^2)) return 0   // shoulder ellipse
    if (ny < 0.34 && |nx| < 0.34*(1 - ny/0.34) + 0.12) return 0   // arch notch
    return 1
```

**Carve on impact.** Convert world impact to grid space, then radially destroy with a jittered boundary so erosion looks organic rather than circular:

```
carve(gx, gy, r, seed):
    for cy in [gy-r, gy+r], cx in [gx-r, gx+r]:
        d2 = (cx-gx)² + (cy-gy)²
        rr = r * (0.72 + 0.56 * hash01(cx, cy, seed))     // ragged edge
        if d2 <= rr*rr && occ[i]: occ[i]=0; freedCells++
    if freedCells: markDirty()                            // instance list rebuilt once/frame
```

Carve radii: player bullet **1.6 cells**, bomb **2.1**, invader body contact **4.0** (invaders erase bunkers they touch — canonical). Each carve emits `freedCells` debris particles, capped at 14, priority 40.

The instance buffer is **compacted only when dirty**, once per frame maximum, not per carve. Freed cells spawn debris; `mesh.count` is reduced rather than reallocating the buffer.

### 1.10 Mystery UFO — The Authentic Score Table

Spawn timer `25.6 s ± 4 s` (arcade-accurate cadence), suppressed when `alive < 8`. Traverses at 8.5 u/s from the side opposite the player's current half.

The arcade's UFO score is **not random** — it is a lookup indexed by the player's cumulative shot counter:

```
UFO_TABLE = [100,50,50,100,150,100,100,50,300,100,100,100,50,150,100]
score = UFO_TABLE[ playerShotCount % 15 ]
```

Firing your 23rd shot at the UFO always yields 300. We implement the table verbatim, and the HUD's shot counter subtly advertises it to players who notice — a deep-cut reward for arcade literacy.

### 1.11 Scoring, Lives, Progression

| Event | Points |
|---|---|
| Row 0 (small "squid") | 30 |
| Rows 1–2 ("crab") | 20 |
| Rows 3–4 ("octopus") | 10 |
| UFO | table above |
| Bomb intercepted | 5 × current combo |

**Combo (modern):** kills within a 1.4 s rolling window increment `combo` (max ×8). Score awarded `= base × (1 + 0.25·(combo−1))`. Combo decay resets on player death.

Lives: 3. Extra life at **1500**, then every 3000. Wave `n` starts formation at `SPAWN_TOP_Y − 0.55·(n−1)`, floored at `3.0`; from wave 9 the floor holds and only `levelScalar` continues to tighten.

### 1.12 Loss & Recovery States

| State | Trigger | Behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| `PLAYER_HIT` | bomb ∩ player AABB | 1.9 s freeze-out: hit-stop 0.22, trauma 0.9, ship voxel-shatters, formation halts, music mutes to a low drone |
| `OVERRUN` | `origin.y − maxAliveRow·SPACING_Y ≤ KILL_LINE_Y` | immediate game over, camera slow-dollies into the lattice |
| `WAVE_CLEAR` | `aliveCount === 0` | 2.2 s: hit-stop 0.14, all bunkers +1 row regen, wave banner, tempo reset |
| `GAME_OVER` | lives exhausted or `OVERRUN` | high-score panel, `Storage` write, restart prompt |

---

## 2. MODERN ENHANCEMENTS — 21 AAA UPGRADES

Each entry names the **exact** Three.js mechanism.

**1 · Bitmap-to-Voxel Procedural Invaders.**
The three invader species and the UFO are authored as the original 11×8 / 12×8 / 16×7 sprite bitmaps stored as `Uint8Array` literals in `content/InvaderBitmaps.js`. At init, `GeometryLab.bitmapToMesh()` runs **greedy rectangle meshing** over each bitmap (scan for maximal axis-aligned runs, then extend vertically while the run matches), collapsing ~34 lit pixels into ~11 boxes. Each rect becomes a `BoxGeometry(w·s, h·s, DEPTH)`, `.translate()`-ed into place, and the set is fused with `BufferGeometryUtils.mergeGeometries([...], false)`. Result: **one geometry, one draw call per species**, bevel-read achieved by a 0.94 inner-scale shell pass. Zero image files, and the silhouette is pixel-exact to 1978.

**2 · Two-Pose March Animation.**
The arcade alternated two sprite frames per step. We hold **two merged geometries per species** and swap which `InstancedMesh` a given lattice index writes into on `animFrame ^= 1` — i.e. two `InstancedMesh` objects per species, and the march step redistributes instance counts between them. No morph targets, no skinning, 2 extra draw calls total.

**3 · Neon Projectile Materials via `onBeforeCompile`.**
The directive mandates `MeshStandardMaterial`. We **extend rather than replace**: `mat.onBeforeCompile = (shader) => {...}` injects a `uTime` uniform and patches `#include <emissivemap_fragment>` to add a scrolling longitudinal energy gradient plus a Fresnel rim:

```
totalEmissiveRadiance *= 0.6 + 1.8 * pow(1.0 - abs(dot(vNormal, vViewDir)), 2.2);
totalEmissiveRadiance += uCore * smoothstep(0.42, 0.0, abs(fract(vUv.y*3.0 - uTime*2.4) - 0.5));
```

`mat.customProgramCacheKey = () => 'si-bolt'` prevents shader recompilation churn (the skill's named perf hazard). Still a `MeshStandardMaterial`: full PBR, full lighting, full compliance.

**4 · Motion Trails on Every Fast Body.**
`@shared/vfx/TrailRenderer` ribbon: 16-segment ring buffer sampled at a fixed 60 Hz, width tapering `w(i) = w₀·(1 − i/n)^0.7`, emissive `MeshStandardMaterial`, `depthWrite = false`. Attached to player bolts, all three bomb types, the UFO, and shattered ship debris.

**5 · Pooled Dynamic Lighting (hard budget: 6).**
`LightPool` holds 6 `PointLight`s. Every glow-worthy event requests one with a priority and a lifetime; the pool grants by `priority × (1/(1+distToCamera))`, stealing the lowest-scoring active light when full. Player death and UFO destruction always win. Lights animate `intensity = I₀·(1−t)²` and are *parked* (intensity 0, moved off-screen) rather than added/removed — **adding or removing a light recompiles every material in the scene**, so we never do it after init.

**6 · Reactive Generative Soundtrack Locked to `stepPeriod`.**
`MusicDirector` sequences the four-note descending bass motif (the original's entire soundtrack) with note duration `= stepPeriod`. As invaders die, the music accelerates because it is *literally the same variable*. Layered on top: a sub-bass sine with an exponential pitch envelope, a filtered-noise hat on off-beats above wave 3, and a pad whose `BiquadFilterNode` cutoff maps to `1 − alive/55`. All Web Audio, all synthesized, zero samples.

**7 · Synthesized SFX Library.**
`SFXLibrary` recipes, each a short-lived `OscillatorNode`/`BufferSource` graph torn down on `onended`:
- *bolt*: saw osc 880→2200 Hz over 0.06 s, lowpass sweep, 0.09 s decay
- *invader death*: filtered white noise burst + square sub, ring-modulated at 60 Hz
- *bunker chip*: 12 ms noise, bandpass at 1.8 kHz, tight decay
- *UFO*: continuous FM pair (carrier 520 Hz, modulator 7 Hz, index 90) — the iconic warble
- *player death*: descending sawtooth glide 400→40 Hz over 1.1 s, heavy distortion via `WaveShaperNode`
A procedurally generated impulse response (exponentially decaying noise, 1.4 s) feeds one shared `ConvolverNode` for arena reverb.

**8 · Voxel Death Shatter.**
Invaders do not vanish. On death, the species' greedy-mesh rect list is replayed as up to 11 pooled debris cubes given radial velocity `v = normalize(pos − impact)·(4 + 6·rand)`, angular velocity, gravity `−9`, and a 0.9 s life with an easing scale-to-zero. Debris is `InstancedMesh`-backed and shares the particle budget at priority 60.

**9 · Shockwave Rings.**
Pool of 12 `RingGeometry(0.9, 1.0, 48)` meshes, emissive `MeshStandardMaterial`, `depthWrite=false`, `renderOrder=10`. `scale = R·easeOutCubic(t)`, `opacity = (1−t)^1.6`, oriented to the impact normal. Fired on: invader death (R=1.8), UFO death (R=4.5), bunker hit (R=1.1), player death (R=9.0), wave clear (R=16, from arena centre).

**10 · Parallax Starfield — PBR-Compliant.**
`Points` cannot carry `MeshStandardMaterial`, so stars are **three `InstancedMesh` layers** of `IcosahedronGeometry(1, 0)` (20 tris) at 260 / 180 / 90 instances, scales 0.018 / 0.032 / 0.055, emissive intensities 0.5 / 1.1 / 2.2. Distribution is **stratified jitter** (grid cell + random offset) rather than uniform random, which eliminates the clumping that makes procedural starfields read as noise. Layers translate at `0.02 / 0.05 / 0.11 ×` camera parallax.

**11 · fBm Nebula Backdrop.**
A `PlaneGeometry` at `z = −40` with a 1024² `CanvasTexture` generated once from 5-octave simplex fBm:

```
fbm(p) = Σ(o=0..4) 0.5^o · simplex(p · 2^o)
value  = smoothstep(0.28, 0.86, fbm(p)) 
colour = lerp(#0a0d24, #2b1a5e, value) + #ff2f9a · pow(value, 5.0) · 0.55
```

Used as `emissiveMap` with `emissiveIntensity 0.55`, `roughness 1.0`. The source canvas is `.dispose()`-ed after upload; only the GPU texture survives.

**12 · Holographic Arena Floor & Walls.**
Procedural 512² grid canvas → three derived maps via `PBRMaps.fromHeight()`: a **Sobel-derived normal map** (`n = normalize(vec3(−dH/dx·s, −dH/dy·s, 1))`, packed `n·0.5+0.5`), a roughness map (grid lines glossy at 0.18, field matte at 0.85), and an emissive map (lines only). A scan-line sweep animates `emissiveMap.offset.y` at 0.06 u/s; on each formation drop the emissive intensity pulses `1.0 → 3.4 → 1.0` over 0.35 s.

**13 · Explicit Camera Rig with Tension Dolly.**
Camera is a boundary object, not scattered mutations (skill rule 3). `CameraRig` composes four independent contributions, summed once per frame:
- **base**: `(0, −0.6, 24.5)`, `lookAt(0, 0.4, 0)`, 46° FOV
- **parallax**: `+playerX · 0.11` on X, `+playerX · 0.004` on rotation.z
- **tension dolly**: `descent = clamp((SPAWN_TOP_Y − origin.y)/12, 0, 1)`; `z −= 3.2·descent`, `fov −= 3.5·descent`. As the aliens close in, the camera closes in. Nobody notices consciously; everybody feels it.
- **shake**: additive offset from `CameraShake` (§4.1)

`updateProjectionMatrix()` is called **only** when FOV actually changes beyond 0.01°.

**14 · Wave Warp-In.**
Formation materialises with per-instance staggered elastic scale-in: `delay = (col + row)·0.028 s`, `scale = easeOutBack(t, 1.7)`, accompanied by an upward-sweeping emissive band and a rising synth arpeggio. Total 1.5 s. Input is accepted from t=0.6 s so the player never waits on a cutscene.

**15 · Bomb Interception Duel.**
Player bolts destroy plunger and squiggly bombs. Interception → 8 sparks (priority 30), 0.02 s hit-stop only if `combo ≥ 3`, `5 × combo` points, and a distinct high metallic *tink*. This converts the classic's passive dodging into an active, skill-expressive defensive verb without altering any canonical rule (rolling bombs remain invulnerable).

**16 · UFO Scan Beam.**
While alive, the UFO projects a `ConeGeometry` (open-ended, 14 radial segments) downward: `MeshStandardMaterial` with an animated emissive stripe texture, `transparent`, `opacity 0.22`, `depthWrite false`, `side: DoubleSide`. The beam sweeps ±0.22 rad and briefly *illuminates* whatever it crosses via a pooled `SpotLight` — the volumetric read comes from bloom on the cone plus real light on the geometry beneath, no ray-marching required.

**17 · Adaptive Difficulty Director.**
Tracks a rolling `accuracy = hits/shots` and `timeToClear`. Nudges `bombProbability` by `×(0.82 … 1.24)` and UFO cadence by `∓15%`, clamped so it can never exceed the authored wave curve by more than one wave-equivalent. Fully disabled in an "Arcade Purist" toggle in the pause menu.

**18 · Floating Score Text.**
Pool of 14 DOM nodes. World position → `vec.project(camera)` → CSS `translate3d`. Rises 44 px over 0.85 s with `cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1)`, scales `1.35 → 1.0`, fades from 1 → 0 after 55% of life. Combo ≥ 3 switches the class to a hotter neon and adds a `×N` suffix. DOM (not sprites) because it is always crisp, costs zero draw calls, and cannot be blown out by bloom.

**19 · Dual-Input with Haptics.**
`InputManager` polls keyboard (WASD + Arrows) and the Gamepad API every frame, merging into one `ActionMap` (`moveX` float, `fire`/`pause`/`confirm` edge-detected). Gamepad: 0.18 radial deadzone, then `t = (|a|−dz)/(1−dz)`, response curve `t²·sign(a)`. `gamepad.vibrationActuator.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on fire (weak 0.12/60 ms), invader kill (0.3/90 ms), player death (1.0/420 ms). The active device is auto-detected from last-input and swaps the on-screen prompt glyphs.

**20 · Adaptive Quality Governor.**
`Perf` maintains a 60-frame rolling mean. Above 20 ms it steps down: (1) bloom target resolution ÷2, (2) particle cap 500 → 320, (3) film grain off, (4) shadows off. Below 12 ms for 180 consecutive frames it steps back up, with hysteresis so it can never oscillate. Current tier is visible in the F3 `DebugPanel` alongside draw calls, triangles, live particles, and pool high-water marks.

**21 · Accessibility & Browser Safety.**
`prefers-reduced-motion` clamps trauma to 0.25×, disables chromatic aberration and hit-stop, and replaces camera shake with a brief vignette flash. A colour-blind-safe palette toggle shifts the invader/bomb hue split from red/cyan to blue/amber. `webglcontextlost` is captured (`e.preventDefault()`), the loop suspends, a glass "Reinitialising renderer" panel appears, and `webglcontextrestored` rebuilds all GPU-side resources from the still-intact simulation state — which is only possible *because* simulation state never lived inside Three.js objects. `visibilitychange` and `blur` auto-pause and suspend the `AudioContext`.

---

## 3. GRAPHICS PIPELINE

### 3.1 Renderer Configuration

```js
new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, powerPreference: 'high-performance',
                          stencil: false, depth: true })
renderer.outputColorSpace     = THREE.SRGBColorSpace
renderer.toneMapping          = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping
renderer.toneMappingExposure  = 1.06
renderer.shadowMap.enabled    = true
renderer.shadowMap.type       = THREE.PCFSoftShadowMap
renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(devicePixelRatio, 2))
```

`antialias: false` is deliberate — MSAA does nothing for a post-processed pipeline; SMAA runs in the composer after bloom instead.

### 3.2 Post-Processing Stack

Render targets use `THREE.HalfFloatType` so bloom has real HDR headroom above 1.0.

| # | Pass | Configuration |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `RenderPass` | scene + `CameraRig.camera` |
| 2 | `UnrealBloomPass` | strength **0.85**, radius **0.55**, threshold **0.72** |
| 3 | `ChromaticAberrationPass` | custom `ShaderPass`; offset `0.0012 + 0.0055·trauma²`, radial from centre |
| 4 | `FilmGrainPass` | custom `ShaderPass`; grain 0.045 (hash noise), scanline 0.055 @ 900 lines, vignette 0.55 |
| 5 | `SMAAPass` | edge AA on the composited image |
| 6 | `OutputPass` | tone-mapping + colour-space conversion, always last |

**Threshold 0.72 is the whole aesthetic contract.** Neon emitters are authored with `emissiveIntensity ≥ 1.4` so they clear it; hull, bunker, and floor materials are authored below 0.5 so they never bloom. This gives *selective* glow with a single global bloom pass and no second render target — and, per the skill's readability rule, it guarantees bombs and bolts stay legible against a glowing background because the background is deliberately *below* threshold.

Bloom resolution is `size/2` at tier 0 and `size/4` at tier 1+. On resize, `composer.setSize()` and `bloomPass.resolution.set()` are called — targets are **resized, never recreated**, so no allocation churn.

### 3.3 Lighting Rig

| Light | Config |
|---|---|
| `HemisphereLight` | sky `#1b2a5a`, ground `#080810`, intensity 0.38 |
| `DirectionalLight` (key) | `#cfe4ff`, 1.15, at `(6, 14, 9)`, `castShadow`, ortho frustum tightly fit to the arena AABB, `mapSize 2048`, `bias −0.0006`, `normalBias 0.02` |
| `SpotLight` (fill) | `#ff3a8c`, 0.45, from below-front, no shadow — the retro-futurist magenta underglow |
| 6 × pooled `PointLight` | §2.5 — allocated at init, never added/removed at runtime |

Total: **9 lights, fixed for the session.** Materials compile once.

### 3.4 Procedural Generation Mathematics

**Simplex fBm** (`@shared/procgen/SimplexNoise` + `PRNG` seeded per-wave for reproducible layouts):
`fbm(p) = Σ_{o=0}^{4} 0.5^o · simplex(p·2^o)`, normalised by `Σ0.5^o = 1.9375`.

**Sobel normal map from a height canvas:**
```
dX = (h[x+1,y-1] + 2h[x+1,y] + h[x+1,y+1]) − (h[x-1,y-1] + 2h[x-1,y] + h[x-1,y+1])
dY = (h[x-1,y+1] + 2h[x,y+1] + h[x+1,y+1]) − (h[x-1,y-1] + 2h[x,y-1] + h[x+1,y-1])
n  = normalize(vec3(−dX·strength, −dY·strength, 1.0))
rgb = n·0.5 + 0.5
```

**Idle bob (visual only):** `bob(i).y = 0.045·sin(t·2.1 + (col+row·1.7))`, `bob(i).z = 0.03·sin(t·1.4 + col·0.9)`. Excluded from collision by construction — collision reads `worldPos()` without the bob term.

**Greedy rectangle meshing** (bitmap → boxes): for each unvisited lit pixel, extend maximally right while lit-and-unvisited, then extend the whole run downward while every pixel in the run matches; mark visited; emit one rect. Reduces the 11×8 "squid" from 34 boxes to **11**.

**Stratified star placement:** for a target of `N` stars, use a `⌈√N⌉²` grid over the layer plane and place one star per cell at `cell + (rand, rand)·cellSize`, discarding the excess. Even coverage, no clumps, no rejection loop.

### 3.5 Material Budget

`MaterialLibrary` caches by key; the entire game ships **11 unique materials**:
`hull`, `hullAccent`, `invaderA/B/C` (emissive-tinted variants), `bolt`, `bombPlunger`, `bombRoll`, `bunker`, `floor`, `nebula`, `star`, `ring`, `beam`. Every one is `MeshStandardMaterial`. Unique-material count is the #1 cause of draw-call inflation per the skill's perf reference; capping it at init is the mitigation.

### 3.6 Performance Budget

| Metric | Target | Ceiling |
|---|---|---|
| Draw calls | ≤ 42 | 60 |
| Triangles | ≤ 90 k | 150 k |
| Unique materials | 13 | 16 |
| Live particles | — | **500 (hard)** |
| Frame time @1080p | ≤ 8 ms | 16.6 ms |
| Sim tick | 120 Hz | — |

