## 4. VFX IMPLEMENTATION — PRIORITY LOGIC

All six mandatory shared effects are routed through the single `VFXDirector` façade. Game code **never** touches `ParticleManager`, `CameraShake`, `HitStop`, or `Shockwave` directly:

```js
VFX.impact({ position, normal, power, colour, tags });
```

`power ∈ [0,1]` is normalised impact energy; one shared curve maps it to trauma, particle count, hit-stop, and shockwave radius. This is why every title in the roster will *feel* like the same product.

### 4.1 Camera Shake — Trauma Model

```
trauma  = clamp(trauma + amount · velocityScale, 0, 1)
trauma -= DECAY · unscaledDt                      // DECAY = 1.4
shake   = trauma²                                 // squared: small hits stay subtle, big hits dominate

offset.x = MAX_OFF · shake · simplex(t·22, 0.0)
offset.y = MAX_OFF · shake · simplex(t·22, 41.7)
roll     = MAX_ROLL· shake · simplex(t·17, 93.1)

MAX_OFF = 0.62 u ,  MAX_ROLL = 0.055 rad
velocityScale = clamp(impactSpeed / 22.0, 0.5, 2.0)
```

Simplex noise (not `Math.random()`) is essential — random shake reads as strobing; coherent noise reads as a physical camera being hit. Shake integrates on the **unscaled** clock so it keeps moving during hit-stop, which is precisely what makes a freeze frame feel violent instead of broken.

| Event | Trauma |
|---|---|
| Player fires | 0.045 |
| Bomb intercepted | 0.10 |
| Bunker hit | 0.12 |
| Invader killed | 0.18 |
| Formation drop | 0.06 |
| UFO destroyed | 0.35 |
| Wave clear | 0.45 |
| **Player death** | **0.90** |

### 4.2 Hit-Stop — Priority-Max Queue

```
request(duration, priority):
    if (priority > active.priority || duration > active.remaining):
        active = { duration, remaining: duration, priority }

timeScale = remaining > 0
            ? lerp(FREEZE_SCALE, 1.0, smoothstep(0, RELEASE, duration - remaining))
            : 1.0
FREEZE_SCALE = 0.06 ,  RELEASE = 0.55 · duration
```

Requests do **not** stack (stacking produces slow-motion mush); the strongest wins and eased release restores momentum.

| Event | Duration | Priority | Gate |
|---|---|---|---|
| Bomb intercepted | 0.02 s | 1 | only if `combo ≥ 3` |
| Invader killed | 0.03 s | 2 | only if `combo ≥ 3` — otherwise the constant march would stutter |
| Bunker breach | 0.04 s | 3 | only when a bunker's last row falls |
| UFO destroyed | 0.09 s | 6 | always |
| Wave clear | 0.14 s | 8 | always |
| **Player death** | **0.22 s** | **10** | always |

The combo gate on routine kills is the critical tuning decision: freezing on *every* kill in a 55-kill game would be nauseating. Hit-stop must stay rare enough to mean something.

### 4.3 Particle Manager — Hard Cap 500

One `InstancedMesh` of `IcosahedronGeometry(1, 0)`, `count = 500`, emissive `MeshStandardMaterial` with per-instance colour via `InstancedBufferAttribute`. Dead particles are collapsed to zero scale, never removed. Backing store is a **struct-of-arrays** (`Float32Array` for pos/vel/life/size, `Uint8Array` for priority) — no per-particle objects, no GC pressure.

**Eviction policy at saturation:** a request for `n` particles at priority `p` receives `min(n, free)` immediately. If `free < n`, the manager scans for live particles with `priority < p` **and** `age > 0.1 s`, recycling oldest-first until satisfied or exhausted. A player-death burst therefore always reads at full strength even mid-firefight, and thruster embers are always the first thing sacrificed.

| Emitter | Count | Priority | Life | Behaviour |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Thruster embers | 1–2/frame | 10 | 0.35 s | trails behind ship, first to be evicted |
| Bomb pop | 8 | 30 | 0.45 s | radial, fast decay |
| Bunker chips | ≤ 14 | 40 | 0.9 s | gravity `−11`, ground bounce `e = 0.35` |
| Invader shatter | ≤ 11 | 60 | 0.9 s | voxel debris, angular velocity, gravity `−9` |
| UFO destruction | 40 | 80 | 1.3 s | two-tone, upward bias, slow drag |
| **Player death** | **72** | **100** | **1.7 s** | omnidirectional, always fully granted |

Integration: `v *= (1 − DRAG·dt)`, `v.y −= G·dt`, `p += v·dt`, `size = s₀·(1 − t)^0.6`, emissive `= e₀·(1 − t)²`. Particles run on the **scaled** clock so they hang beautifully in a freeze frame.

### 4.4 Effect Priority Under Simultaneous Events

When multiple impacts land in one tick: trauma **accumulates** (clamped to 1), hit-stop takes the **maximum**, particles are granted in **descending priority order**, shockwaves take the **top 3** requests (pool of 12, ~4 in flight), and floating text merges into a single combo popup rather than overlapping.

